findings.breaking_input:
A merge_time bats test whose body contains a `}` line that is part of a here-doc, e.g. `@test "x" {
cat <<'EOF'
}
skip
EOF
}`. The region runs until the next `@test` line, so it includes the `}` and `skip` after it; the skip word is outside a comment, so it is flagged -- safe. The hole is the opposite: a body with `if true; then skip; fi` -- the line `  skip "x"` is not a comment, so ` skip ` matches and flags -- good. The real hole: a skip expressed as `skip` with a leading `#` comment char on the SAME line, e.g. `# skip` is ignored, but `skip # comment` still matches. What does NOT match: `bats_require_minimum_version 1.5.0` then `skip` defined as a local function? The region scan uses `/ skip /` on any non-comment line, so a test that defines a function `skip_quietly` or a string `"skip this"` is a false gap. The true bypass: `skip` word inside a shell comment preceded by whitespace, e.g. `  # skip` is ignored because the line starts with `#`. But `  # skip` after a `#` is a comment line and is correctly ignored. A line `foo # skip` is not a comment line (starts with `foo`) so flagged -- false. The real untested: `skip` in a `bats` file loaded as part of a helper via `load`, not in the `@test` body; the region does not include helper files, so a test that calls a helper that skips passes the check. Concrete: `@test "x" { skip_helper; }` where `skip_helper` in a loaded file does `skip`. gate-parity sees no `skip` word in the region and accepts.
